The last speaker names engineering as his best financial decision, even though he works in cybersecurity and privacy rather than a conventional engineering role. That belongs in the book’s portable-skill thread. A credential matters here not as status, but as training in problem solving that can travel across industries: $\text{formal training}
\longrightarrow
\text{problem-solving method}
\longrightarrow
\text{career mobility}.$ Lecture 146 therefore ends where its income question was always pointing: the durable object is not the number alone, but the structure that produced it and the capability that can keep producing after the immediate job, client, or month changes.

